    <p>The three new material rows use product examples rather than fixed rock densities. <a href="https://landcarestone.com/sand-gravel/3-4-crushed-stone-2/" rel="noopener noreferrer">LandCare lists 2,800 lb/yd³ for 3/4-inch crushed granite</a>, while <a href="https://www.landscapecentre.com/products/bulk-materials/gravel" rel="noopener noreferrer">Landscape Centre lists about 2,600 lb/yd³ for its clear-crush granite</a>. <a href="https://dressertraprock.com/price-schedule/" rel="noopener noreferrer">Dresser Trap Rock lists an average 2,600 lb/yd³</a>, with <a href="https://shop.ficksupply.com/product/trap-small-34-1" rel="noopener noreferrer">Fick listing 2,700 lb/yd³</a> for another trap product. <a href="https://southbaymaterials.com/drain-rock-3-4" rel="noopener noreferrer">South Bay lists 2,500 lb/yd³ for its washed drain rock</a>, while <a href="https://shop.three-z.com/index.php?product_id=58&amp;route=product%2Fproduct" rel="noopener noreferrer">Three Z lists roughly 2,800 lb/yd³ for washed #57 gravel</a>. The coverage columns are geometric estimates for flat layers; use the <a href="/drain-rock-calculator">drain-rock trench tool</a> for pipe and top-cover deductions.</p>

    <p>The DG row starts at 2,500 lb per loose cubic yard, or 1.25 US short tons. It is a planning preset, not an ASTM value. <a href="https://www.gravelshop.com/shop/calculate-cubic-yard-feet-ton.asp?groupid=30&amp;productid=819" rel="noopener noreferrer">Gravelshop lists one ½-inch-minus DG at 2,410 lb/yd³</a>, while <a href="https://centraltexasmaterials.com/materials/crushed-granite" rel="noopener noreferrer">Central Texas Materials lists its DG at 2,700 lb/yd³</a>. The example range rounds those two supplier figures to about 2,400–2,700 lb/yd³ (89–100 lb/ft³). Use the measured or quoted density for your specific product.</p>

    <p>The lava-rock row uses 1,500 lb per loose cubic yard as a planning example, not a fixed weight for all scoria. <a href="https://www.landscapecentre.com/products/bulk-materials/lava-rock">Landscape Centre lists 1,250 lb/yd³</a>, <a href="https://www.palmercokingcoal.com/products/lava-rock">Palmer Coking Coal lists about 1,300–1,500 lb/yd³</a> for its lava products, and <a href="https://www.sutherlandscape.com/products/lava-rock-black/">Sutherland lists 1,420 lb/yd³</a> for black lava. Enter the current supplier figure in the <a href="/lava-rock-coverage-calculator">lava rock coverage calculator</a> when converting tons to area.</p>

    <p>The marble-chip row uses 2,700 lb per loose cubic yard as an example. <a href="https://cumminlandscapesupply.com/marblechips-gravel">Cummin Landscape Supply lists about 2,700 lb/yd³</a>, while <a href="https://www.gravelshop.com/shop/calculate-cubic-yard-feet-ton.asp?groupid=86&amp;productid=529">Gravelshop lists 2,410 lb/yd³</a> for another white-chip product. The row's 89–100 lb/ft³ range roughly spans those two products; the coverage figures use the 2,700 lb/yd³ example and exclude extra ordering allowance. Use the <a href="/marble-chips-calculator">marble chips calculator</a> to enter the weight on your supplier quote.</p>

    <p>The beach-pebble row starts from <a href="https://www.westseattlestone.com/product/mexican-beach-pebbles-1-2-black">West Seattle Stone's 2,600 lb/yd³ listing</a>. <a href="https://rosevillerocksupply.com/products/mixed-mexican-beach-pebbles-1-2/">Roseville Rock Supply lists about 1.3–1.4 short tons/yd³</a>, giving an illustrative range near 96–104 lb/ft³. The area figures are geometric estimates at the 2,600 lb/yd³ example with no allowance. Enter your product's actual weight in the <a href="/beach-pebble-calculator">beach pebble calculator</a>.</p>

    <p>The egg-rock row uses <a href="https://mainslandscapesupply.com/product/egg-rock/">Main's Landscape Supply's 2,800 lb/yd³ egg-rock listing</a>; <a href="https://cumminlandscapesupply.com/Egg-Rock">Cummin Landscape Supply lists about 2,400 lb/yd³</a> for another grade. That is roughly 89–104 lb/ft³. The row shows geometric coverage at the 2,800 lb/yd³ example. The <a href="/egg-rock-calculator">egg rock calculator</a> lets you enter the current weight for your product.</p>

    <h2>Why Densities Vary: The Three Field Factors</h2>
    <p>
      Experienced civil engineers and contractors understand that bulk density is not a single static number. True field densities fluctuate based on three key physical variables:
    </p>

    <h3>1. Mineral Specific Gravity</h3>
    <p>
      The parent geology of the quarry dictates base mineral density. Igneous basalt and granite naturally possess higher solid mineral specific gravities (2.65 to 2.80) than porous sedimentary limestone (2.50 to 2.65) or volcanic scoria (1.40 to 1.70).
    </p>

    <h3>2. Moisture Content (Rainfall Absorption)</h3>
    <p>
      Aggregates stored in outdoor quarry stockpiles absorb surface moisture during rain events. Wet crushed stone can weigh <strong>5 to 8 percent more per cubic yard</strong> than dry stone. When ordering by certified scale weight after heavy rainfall, slight volumetric adjustments occur.
    </p>

    <h3>3. Loose vs Compacted State</h3>
    <p>
      Dense-graded aggregates such as crusher run or decomposed granite can occupy less volume after compaction. That changes their bulk density, but the change depends on gradation, moisture, equipment, and site conditions. Use a supplier's loose-to-finished allowance when estimating material for a specified finished depth.
    </p>
